# ::game — a made game's spec

::summary
A game made in CloudSurf keeps its spec as one `::game` block: the engine and its version on the opener, then a flat key list the engine reads. surf-parse reads the keys and draws them as one card; it never evaluates a key and never checks one against an engine. The platform's engine reads the body.
::

## The grammar

```surf
::game[engine=runner version=1]
title: Fox Run
character: fox
track: desert
speed: 1.2
lanes: 3
obstacles:
  - cone (low, jump)
  - bar (high, slide)
  - cactus (tall, lane)
::
```

| Attribute | What |
|---|---|
| `engine` | The engine that reads the body: `runner`, `wilds`, or any engine the platform offers. Kept as written. |
| `version` | The version of that engine's keys, a whole number. |

The body is a flat key list:

- `key: value` is a scalar key. The value is kept as written; surf-parse never reads it as a number.
- `key:` with nothing after the colon opens a list; each `- item` (or `* item`) line after it, at any indent, is one item. A list with no items reads as an empty value.
- A key is one word of letters, digits, `_`, `-` or `.`. Blank lines are skipped.

The parsed block is `Block::Game { engine, version, fields, body }`. `fields` holds the keys in order, each a `GameField { key, value }` whose value is `GameValue::Scalar` or `GameValue::List`. `body` is the spec exactly as written, so a host hands it to its engine unchanged.

## What surf-parse does not judge

- **An unknown engine parses.** `::game[engine=platformer]` is a `Block::Game` with `engine = "platformer"`; whether that engine exists is the platform's call.
- **A missing engine parses.** `::game` with no `engine=` is a `Block::Game` with `engine = ""`, and lint `L049` warns.
- **A stray line parses.** A body line that is neither `key: value` nor a `- item` under a `key:` stays in `body`, is left off the card, and lint `L049` names its line.

Nothing about a `::game` is ever a parse error.

## The card

```html
<section class="surfdoc-game" data-engine="runner" data-version="1">
  <header class="surfdoc-game-head">
    <h3 class="surfdoc-game-title">Fox Run</h3>
    <span class="surfdoc-game-engine">Runner</span>
  </header>
  <dl class="surfdoc-game-keys"><dt>character</dt><dd>fox</dd> …</dl>
  <div class="surfdoc-game-list">
    <span class="surfdoc-game-list-key">obstacles</span>
    <ul class="surfdoc-game-items"><li>cone (low, jump)</li> …</ul>
  </div>
</section>
```

(The renderer writes it on one line; it is spread out here to read.)

- The heading is the `title:` key, else "Game".
- The engine's word: "Runner" for `runner`, "Surflings world" for `wilds`, any other engine as written; nothing when there is no engine.
- `data-engine` and `data-version` appear when the opener sets them.
- The other scalar keys form the definition list; each list key is a list under its name, after it.
- Every value is escaped text. The card has no script, no inline style and no event handler; the stylesheet carries its look in tokens. The constructive DOM path builds the same tree byte for byte.

## Everywhere else

| Target | What it draws |
|---|---|
| Native (schema v18) | An open `Details` card: the title as its heading, then the engine line (`Runner · version 1`) and the keys as Markdown. No new variant; the apps need nothing new. |
| Markdown | `**Fox Run** · Runner · version 1`, then `- key: value` lines and each list's items indented under its key. |
| Terminal | The title in bold, the engine line dimmed, the key lines indented. |
| LaTeX, Typst, PDF | The title in bold, the engine line, the keys as a list (a list key's items as a list inside it). |
| Email | The Markdown lines. |

The serializer writes `::game[engine="runner" version=1]` and the body back verbatim, so a round trip is a fixed point.
